<header><p class="st-kicker">prince bishopric · Germany and Central Europe</p><h1 class="st-title">Prince-Bishopric of Merseburg</h1><p class="st-meta">968 CE – 1565 CE · capital Merseburg</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">An Ottonian bishopric on the Saale whose bishop Thietmar wrote a famous chronicle, later absorbed into Saxony after the Reformation.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Otto I founded the bishopric of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/merseburg">Merseburg</a> in 968 to fulfil a vow made before the Battle of Lechfeld, as a suffragan of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/magdeburg">Magdeburg</a>. It was dissolved in 981, its lands divided among neighbours, but <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-ii-holy-roman-emperor">Henry II</a> restored it in 1004. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishops</a> built a cathedral beside the royal palace at Merseburg.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">Bishop</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thietmar-of-merseburg">Thietmar of Merseburg</a>, from 1009 to 1018, wrote a chronicle that is a key source on the Ottonian empire, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavs">Slavs</a> and early <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a>. The bishops ruled a small territory along the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saale">Saale</a>. The cathedral library preserves the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/merseburg-charms">Merseburg Charms</a>, the only surviving <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/old-high-german">Old High German</a> pagan spells.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/merseburg">Merseburg</a> came under the influence of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-wettin">Wettin</a> rulers of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxony">Saxony</a>. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protestant-reformation">Reformation</a>, a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxons">Saxon</a> prince became administrator in 1561, and from 1565 the see was effectively a Saxon possession. From 1656 to 1738 it formed the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/duchy-of-saxe-merseburg">Duchy of Saxe-Merseburg</a>, before reverting to the electorate and passing to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a> in 1815.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/prince-bishopric-of-merseburg.jpg" alt="Prince-Bishopric of Merseburg" width="303" height="263"><figcaption>Image: Gustav Droysen · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Osterland_1250.PNG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>968 CE – 1565 CE</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/10th-century">10th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>prince bishopric</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>theocratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Merseburg</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Thietmar of Merseburg</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Catholic Christianity, later Lutheranism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Middle High German, Latin</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Hochstift Merseburg</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1266&amp;focus=prince-bishopric-of-merseburg" aria-label="Prince-Bishopric of Merseburg on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4952 399 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Prince-Bishopric of Merseburg lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5272 655L5229 694M5272 655L5312 664"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5229 694h0M5312 664h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5272 655h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5272 655h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Merseburg. <a href="/atlas?year=1266&amp;focus=prince-bishopric-of-merseburg">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
